The question doesn't name a framework, so here is what the docs cover for Next.js (App Router) and Angular.

## Next.js

Add a `loading.js` file next to your `page.js`. Next.js wraps the page in a `<Suspense>` boundary and uses your loading component as the fallback. [1]

```tsx
// app/dashboard/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  // You can add any UI inside Loading, including a Skeleton.
  return <LoadingSkeleton />
}
```
[3]

The docs mention spinners and skeletons as options for the loading UI. [3] When the page finishes loading, its HTML replaces the fallback, and the layout renders immediately. [1]

Caveats:
- `loading.js` doesn't wrap the `layout.js`, `template.js` or `error.js` in the same segment. [3]
- If the layout accesses uncached or runtime data (e.g. `cookies()`, `headers()`, uncached fetches), `loading.js` won't show a fallback for it. To get instant navigation, move that fetching into `page.js` or wrap it in its own `<Suspense>` boundary. [3]

## Angular

If you use data resolvers, navigation is blocked while they run, so the user sees a delay before the new route appears. [5] You can listen to router state and show an indicator during that time:

```ts
import {Component, computed, inject} from '@angular/core';
import {Router, RouterOutlet} from '@angular/router';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [RouterOutlet],
  template: `
    @if (isNavigating()) {
      <div class="loading-bar">Loading...</div>
    }
    <router-outlet />
  `,
})
export class App {
  private router = inject(Router);
  isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation());
}
```
[5]

Alternatively, to render components immediately with skeleton or loading indicators, use non-blocking route resources. [5]

The provided docs don't cover React Native, so I can't say how to do this there.

Use the `useSearchParams` hook from `next/navigation`. It's a Client Component hook that returns a read-only `URLSearchParams` object [1].

```tsx
'use client'

import { useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation'

export default function SearchBar() {
  const searchParams = useSearchParams()

  const search = searchParams.get('search')

  // URL -> `/dashboard?search=my-project`
  // `search` -> 'my-project'
  return <>Search: {search}</>
}
```
[1]

**Alternative for page components:** If your Client Component is a `page`, you can read the `searchParams` prop, which is a promise, using React's `use` function [2][5].

```tsx
'use client'
import { use } from 'react'

export default function Page({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const filters = use(searchParams).filters
}
```
[5]

Differences to note:
- The `searchParams` prop is a plain JavaScript object, not a `URLSearchParams` instance [5].
- Repeated keys become arrays, e.g. `/shop?a=1&a=2` gives `{ a: ['1', '2'] }` [5].
- Using the `searchParams` prop opts the page into dynamic rendering at request time [5].

Call `redirect()` from `next/navigation` in the Server Component when the check fails. It throws a `NEXT_REDIRECT` error and terminates rendering of that route segment [2].

```tsx
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'

async function fetchTeam(id: string) {
  const res = await fetch('https://...')
  if (!res.ok) return undefined
  return res.json()
}

export default async function Profile({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params
  const team = await fetchTeam(id)

  if (!team) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  // ...
}
```
[2]

You don't need `return redirect()`, because `redirect` uses the TypeScript `never` type [2].

For a sign-in check specifically, the authentication guide with Cache Components shows the same pattern: read the session, and call `redirect('/login')` if there's no `userId` [5].

```tsx
export async function getCurrentUser(): Promise<User> {
  'use cache: private'

  const { userId } = await getSession()
  if (!userId) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  const user = await findUserById(userId)
  if (!user) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  return { id: user.id, name: user.name }
}
```
[5]

The `redirect()` calls throw to interrupt rendering rather than return a value, so they aren't cached [5].

**Alternative:** To redirect before rendering, use `NextResponse.redirect` in Proxy. It runs after `redirects` in `next.config.js` and before rendering [3]. Proxy is suited to optimistic checks that only read the session from the cookie, not the database [4].

Wrap a folder name in square brackets to create a dynamic segment. For `/blog/my-first-post`, create `app/blog/[slug]/page.js`, where `[slug]` is the Dynamic Segment. [1][5]

## Reading the slug

The dynamic segment is passed to your page as the `params` prop, which is a Promise, so `await` it:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  return <div>My Post: {slug}</div>
}
```
[1]

Visiting `/blog/a` gives `params` of `{ slug: 'a' }`, so `/blog/my-first-post` gives `{ slug: 'my-first-post' }`. [1]

## Using the slug to fetch content

You can use the value to load data for that page:

```tsx
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[5]

## Where else `params` is available

Dynamic segments are also passed as `params` to `layout`, `route`, and `generateMetadata` functions, not just `page`. [1]

Yes. Return the full list of paths from `generateStaticParams` and Next.js will statically render every path at build time [5].

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
[5]

`generateStaticParams` works with dynamic route segments, so each returned `params` object produces a statically generated version of the page [4].

Every prerendered page adds build work and output that has to be stored and deployed, and many routes may never be visited before your next deployment. The docs suggest prerendering only the pages that benefit most, such as popular or predictable content, and letting the rest be generated on demand [1]. To do that, return a partial list, for example `posts.slice(0, 10)`. The remaining paths are rendered the first time they're visited [5].

The context covers this for Next.js (App Router) and, for titles only, Angular. It doesn't cover meta descriptions for Angular.

## Next.js

Export a `metadata` object from each `page.js` (or `layout.js`) file. Each page defines its own `title` and `description` [5]:

```tsx
// app/blog/layout.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: '...',
}

export default function Layout() {}
```
[5]

The same approach applies to `app/about/page.js`, with its own values.

**How values combine across segments:**

- A page's `title` replaces the `title` from a parent layout [1].
- Fields the page doesn't set, such as `openGraph`, are inherited from the layout [1].
- A parent layout can set a `title.template` (with a required `title.default`) to add a prefix or suffix to child page titles [3]:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: '%s | Acme',
    default: 'Acme',
  },
}

// app/about/page.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'About',
}

// Output: <title>About | Acme</title>
```
[3]

- To ignore the parent template for a specific page, use `title.absolute` [2]:

```tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    absolute: 'About',
  },
}

// Output: <title>About</title>
```
[2]

## Angular

Set a `title` on each route in your route config. Angular updates the page title when the route activates [4]:

```ts
const routes: Routes = [
  { path: '', component: Home, title: 'Home Page' },
  { path: 'about', component: About, title: 'About Us' },
];
```
[4]

The title can also be set dynamically with a `ResolveFn`, or through a service extending `TitleStrategy` [4].

**Not covered:** the Angular docs provided don't describe setting a meta description per route.

Import `cookies` from `next/headers`, `await` it (it's an async function), and call `.get()` on the result:

```tsx
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  const theme = cookieStore.get('theme')
  return '...'
}
```
[1][3]

Reading works in Server Components because the browser sends cookie data to the server in the HTTP request headers [2]. Setting or deleting cookies is not supported during Server Component rendering. To modify cookies, use a Server Function or a Route Handler [2].

Create a Route Handler with the `route.ts` (or `route.js`) file convention inside the `app/` directory. Route Handlers are public HTTP endpoints that use the Web `Request` and `Response` APIs. [3][4]

## Basic JSON endpoint

```ts filename="app/api/route.ts"
export async function GET() {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}
```

[3] This file handles `GET` requests sent to `/api`. [4]

## Using `NextResponse`

You can also use `NextResponse.json()` to produce a response with a JSON body, optionally setting a status:

```ts filename="app/api/route.ts"
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' }, { status: 500 })
}
```

[2]

## Handling errors

Wrap operations that may throw in `try/catch`, and avoid exposing sensitive information in error messages sent to the client:

```ts filename="/app/api/route.ts"
import { submit } from '@/lib/submit'

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    await submit(request)
    return new Response(null, { status: 204 })
  } catch (reason) {
    const message =
      reason instanceof Error ? reason.message : 'Unexpected error'

    return new Response(message, { status: 500 })
  }
}
```

[4]

## Notes

- Route Handlers are accessible to any client. To restrict access, implement authentication and authorization. [4]
- For a new project, `create-next-app` with the `--api` flag includes an example `route.ts` in the `app/` folder (e.g. `npx create-next-app@latest --api`). [1]

The approach depends on your framework. The docs provided cover Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

**Route-segment level:** add an `error.tsx` file to the segment. It wraps that segment and its nested children in a React error boundary and shows your fallback UI when something throws [4]. It must be a Client Component [2].

```tsx
'use client' // Error boundaries must be Client Components

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function ErrorPage({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: unknown
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[2][4]

Errors bubble up to the nearest parent error boundary, so you can place `error.tsx` files at different levels of the route hierarchy for granular handling [2]. `error.js` does not wrap the `layout.js` or `template.js` above it in the same segment. Use `global-error.js` for root layout errors [4].

**Component level:** use `catchError` to create a boundary that can wrap any part of your component tree [2]:

```tsx
'use client'

import { catchError, type ErrorInfo } from 'next/error'

function ErrorFallback(props: { title: string }, { error, retry }: ErrorInfo) {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <p>{message}</p>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default catchError(ErrorFallback)
```
[2]

Then use the returned component as a wrapper:

```tsx
import ErrorWrapper from '../custom-error-boundary'

export default function Component({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <ErrorWrapper title="Dashboard Error">{children}</ErrorWrapper>
}
```
[3]

**Limitation:** error boundaries don't catch errors in event handlers or async code. Catch those manually and store them with `useState` or `useReducer`, then render a fallback [1].

## Angular

Wrap part of your template in `@boundary`. If a component or directive inside throws during initialization or change detection, Angular renders the `@error` block instead [5]:

```angular-html
@boundary {
  <app-risky-component />
} @error {
  <p>Something went wrong!</p>
}
```
[5]

Put it in a `proxy.ts` (or `proxy.js`) file. Proxy runs code on the server before a request is completed, and based on the incoming request you can rewrite, redirect, modify headers, or respond directly [2]. It runs before routes are rendered and suits authentication, logging, and redirects [2].

**Location:** Create the file in the project root, or inside `src` if you use one, at the same level as `pages` or `app` [2]. If you've customized `pageExtensions` (e.g. to `.page.ts`), name it `proxy.page.ts` or `proxy.page.js` [2].

**Auth redirect example:**

```ts
import { NextResponse, NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { authenticate } from 'auth-provider'

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const isAuthenticated = authenticate(request)

  if (isAuthenticated) {
    return NextResponse.next()
  }

  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/dashboard/:path*',
}
```
[1]

Use `config.matcher` to control which routes Proxy applies to [1][2]. For many routes, you can use a pattern that excludes specific paths, such as API routes and static assets [5]:

```ts
export const config = {
  matcher: ['/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|.*\\.png$).*)'],
}
```
[5]

**Things to know:**
- Proxy runs after `redirects` in `next.config.js` and before rendering [1].
- The `middleware` file convention is deprecated and has been renamed to `proxy` [2].
- Proxy runs on every route, including prefetched ones, so for auth only read the session from the cookie (optimistic checks) and avoid database checks [5].
- Don't rely on shared modules or globals in Proxy. To pass information to your app, use headers, cookies, rewrites, redirects, or the URL [2].

Create a mock with `jest.fn()`, run the code under test, then assert on the calls with matchers or inspect `mock.calls` directly.

## Create the mock and check arguments

Use `.toHaveBeenCalledWith` to verify the mock was called with specific arguments. It compares arguments using the same algorithm as `.toEqual`. [3]

```js
test('registration applies correctly to orange La Croix', () => {
  const beverage = new LaCroix('orange');
  register(beverage);
  const f = jest.fn();
  applyToAll(f);
  expect(f).toHaveBeenCalledWith(beverage);
});
```
[3]

## Check a specific call

- **Nth call:** `.toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(n, ...args)`. The `n` must be a positive integer starting from 1. [4]

```js
test('drinkEach drinks each drink', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  drinkEach(drink, ['lemon', 'octopus']);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(1, 'lemon');
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(2, 'octopus');
});
```
[4]

- **Last call:** `.toHaveBeenLastCalledWith(...args)`. [5]

```js
test('applying to all flavors does mango last', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  applyToAllFlavors(drink);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenLastCalledWith('mango');
});
```
[5]

## Inspect the calls directly

- `mockFn.mock.calls` is an array with one entry per call, and each entry is the array of arguments for that call. For `f('arg1', 'arg2')` followed by `f('arg3', 'arg4')`: [1]

```js
[
  ['arg1', 'arg2'],
  ['arg3', 'arg4'],
];
```
[1]

- `mockFn.mock.lastCall` holds the arguments of the last call (`['arg3', 'arg4']` in the example above), or `undefined` if the function was never called. [2]

Call `jest.mock('axios')` to automatically mock the whole module, then set return values on its methods with `mockResolvedValue`:

```js
import axios from 'axios';
import Users from './users';

jest.mock('axios');

test('should fetch users', () => {
  const users = [{name: 'Bob'}];
  const resp = {data: users};
  axios.get.mockResolvedValue(resp);

  // or you could use the following depending on your use case:
  // axios.get.mockImplementation(() => Promise.resolve(resp))

  return Users.all().then(data => expect(data).toEqual(users));
});
```
[5]

This lets you test code like `axios.get('/users.json')` without hitting the real API, which keeps tests fast and less fragile. [5]

A few things to keep in mind:

- **Scope:** A module mocked with `jest.mock` is mocked only in the file that calls `jest.mock`. Other files importing it get the original implementation. [1]
- **Setup files:** Importing a module in a setup file (via `setupFilesAfterEnv`) prevents mocking of that module and all modules it imports. [1]
- **Custom implementation:** `jest.mock` also accepts a factory function as a second argument. [1]
- **TypeScript:** Use the `jest.Mocked` utility type or the `jest.mocked()` helper to type your mocked modules. [1]

**`.toBe`** checks exact equality for primitives and referential identity for objects. It uses `Object.is` to compare values [1][4]. Two different object instances with identical contents will not pass `.toBe`.

**`.toEqual`** recursively compares all properties of object instances ("deep" equality) [2]. It checks every field of an object or array [4].

The docs show them side by side:

```js
const can1 = {
  flavor: 'grapefruit',
  ounces: 12,
};
const can2 = {
  flavor: 'grapefruit',
  ounces: 12,
};

describe('the La Croix cans on my desk', () => {
  test('have all the same properties', () => {
    expect(can1).toEqual(can2);
  });
  test('are not the exact same can', () => {
    expect(can1).not.toBe(can2);
  });
});
```
[2]

**When to use which**
- Use `.toBe` for primitives or when you want to confirm two references are the same instance [1]. For numbers, `toBe` and `toEqual` are equivalent [5].
- Use `.toEqual` to check the value of an object [4].
- For floating-point numbers, use `.toBeCloseTo` instead of either one [1][5].

**Caveats of `.toEqual`**
- It ignores object keys with `undefined` properties, `undefined` array items, array sparseness, and object type mismatch. Use `.toStrictEqual` if you need those checked [2][3].
- It doesn't do a deep equality check for two errors; only the `message` property is compared [2].

You have a few options in Jest. All of them rely on Jest waiting for the promise to settle.

## Return the promise

Return a promise from your test and Jest waits for it to resolve. If it rejects, the test fails. [3]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', () => {
  return fetchData().then(data => {
    expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
  });
});
```
[3]

## Use `async`/`await`

Put `async` in front of the function passed to `test`, then `await` the call. [2]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
});
```
[2]

## Use `.resolves` / `.rejects`

`.resolves` unwraps the value of a fulfilled promise so you can chain any other matcher. If the promise is rejected, the assertion fails. [4] You can combine it with `async`/`await`:

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).resolves.toBe('peanut butter');
});

test('the fetch fails with an error', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).rejects.toMatch('error');
});
```
[2]

Without `async`/`await`, you must `return` the unwrapped assertion. [4]

```js
test('resolves to lemon', () => {
  return expect(Promise.resolve('lemon')).resolves.toBe('lemon');
});
```
[4]

## Testing rejections

If you expect a promise to reject, use `.catch` and add `expect.assertions` so a fulfilled promise doesn't pass silently. [2]

```js
test('the fetch fails with an error', () => {
  expect.assertions(1);
  return fetchData().catch(error => expect(error).toMatch('error'));
});
```
[2]

## Common pitfall

Always `return` or `await` the promise. If you omit it, the test completes before the promise resolves or rejects. [2]

Use `beforeEach`. It runs a function before each test in the file, which is a good fit for resetting global state used by many tests [1].

```js
const globalDatabase = makeGlobalDatabase();

beforeEach(() => {
  // Clears the database and adds some testing data.
  // Jest will wait for this promise to resolve before running tests.
  return globalDatabase.clear().then(() => {
    return globalDatabase.insert({testData: 'foo'});
  });
});
```

[1]

Details:

- **Async setup:** If the function returns a promise (or is a generator), Jest waits for it to resolve before running the test [1]. You can also take a `done` parameter instead of returning a promise [5].
- **Timeout:** You can pass a `timeout` in milliseconds as a second argument. The default is 5 seconds [1].
- **Scoping:** Inside a `describe` block, `beforeEach` runs only for the tests in that block [1].
- **Teardown:** To clean up after each test, pair it with `afterEach` [5].
- **One-time setup:** Use `beforeAll` if you only need to run setup once before any tests [1].

If the state you're resetting is mocks or modules, there are config options for that:

- `resetMocks: true` (or the `--resetMocks` CLI flag) resets mock state before every test. It's equivalent to calling `jest.resetAllMocks()` before each test. It removes fake implementations but doesn't restore the initial implementation [3][4].
- `resetModules: true` resets the module registry before each test, so module-local state doesn't leak between tests [2].

Yes. Jest supports TypeScript in two ways, and you also need type definitions for the Jest globals.

## Running TypeScript tests

**Via Babel:** Install `@babel/preset-typescript` and add it to the presets in `babel.config.js` (after following the Babel setup). [4]

```bash
npm install --save-dev @babel/preset-typescript
```

```javascript
module.exports = {
  presets: [
    ['@babel/preset-env', {targets: {node: 'current'}}],
    '@babel/preset-typescript',
  ],
};
```

Babel only transpiles, so Jest won't type-check your tests when they run. If you want type-checking, use `ts-jest` or run `tsc` separately, for example as part of your build. [4]

**Via `ts-jest`:** Install `ts-jest`. You may also need to create a configuration file for it. [4]

```bash
npm install --save-dev ts-jest
```

## Typing the Jest globals

There are two options: [4]

1. **`@jest/globals`** ships with Jest and updates whenever you update Jest. Install it and import the APIs explicitly: [2][4]

   ```ts
   import {describe, expect, test} from '@jest/globals';
   import {sum} from './sum';

   describe('sum module', () => {
     test('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {
       expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
     });
   });
   ```

2. **`@types/jest`** provides types for the globals without needing imports. [2]

   ```bash
   npm install --save-dev @types/jest
   ```

   It's a third-party package maintained at DefinitelyTyped, so the latest Jest features may not be covered yet. Match its version to your Jest version as closely as possible, e.g. `27.4.x` for Jest `27.4.0`. [2]

The TypeScript examples in the Jest API docs only work as documented if you explicitly import the Jest APIs from `@jest/globals`. [1][3][5]

Use `jest.spyOn(object, methodName)`. It tracks calls to `object[methodName]`, and by default it also calls the original method, so the behavior isn't replaced. [1]

```js
const video = require('./video');

afterEach(() => {
  // restore the spy created with spyOn
  jest.restoreAllMocks();
});

test('plays video', () => {
  const spy = jest.spyOn(video, 'play');
  const isPlaying = video.play();

  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalled();
  expect(isPlaying).toBe(true);
});
```
[1]

Here the original `play()` still runs and returns `true`, while the spy records the call. [1]

Calling `jest.restoreAllMocks()` in an `afterEach` hook restores the original state after each test. [1]

If your codebase transpiles explicit resource management (e.g. TypeScript >= 5.2), you can use `using spy = jest.spyOn(...)` so the spy is restored automatically when the block exits. [1]

To spy on a getter or setter, pass `'get'` or `'set'` as a third argument. [3]

```js
const spy = jest.spyOn(video, 'play', 'get');
```
[3]

If you later want to replace the behavior, chain `.mockImplementation(...)`. [1]

Use `jest.mock(path, moduleFactory)` with a module factory that returns a constructor function. ES6 classes are constructor functions, so any mock must be a function or a class, and you can build it with mock functions [1]. The factory must be a function that returns a function (a higher-order function) [4].

```javascript
import SoundPlayer from './sound-player';
const mockPlaySoundFile = jest.fn();
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
    return {playSoundFile: mockPlaySoundFile};
  });
});
```
[4]

**Tracking usage**

Because the factory returns `jest.fn()`, you can check constructor calls with `expect(SoundPlayer).toHaveBeenCalled()` or `SoundPlayer.mock.calls` [3]. Methods can be checked through the mock function you return, e.g. `mockPlaySoundFile.mock.calls[0][0]` [5]. Clear the mocks between tests:

```javascript
beforeEach(() => {
  SoundPlayer.mockClear();
  mockPlaySoundFile.mockClear();
});
```
[5]

**Non-default exports**

If the class isn't the default export, return an object keyed by the export name [3]:

```javascript
import {SoundPlayer} from './sound-player';
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return {
    SoundPlayer: jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
      return {playSoundFile: () => {}};
    }),
  };
});
```
[3]

**Hoisting gotchas**

- `jest.mock()` calls are hoisted to the top of the file, so Jest blocks access to out-of-scope variables in the factory. The check is disabled for variables whose names start with `mock`, but you must make sure they're initialized in time [4].
- A variable named `fakePlaySoundFile` will fail with an out-of-scope error [4].
- Defining `const mockSoundPlayer = jest.fn()...` outside and returning it directly from the factory causes a `ReferenceError`, because it's accessed before initialization after hoisting. Wrap it in a function, as in the examples above [4].

