The docs provided cover the Android back button in React Native in two ways: the `BackHandler` API for JS-side handling, and the `DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler` interface when your host Activity isn't a `ReactActivity`.

## Using `BackHandler`

`BackHandler` detects hardware back button presses, lets you register listeners for the system's back action, and lets you control how your app responds. It is Android-only. [3][5]

How subscriptions behave:
- They are called in reverse order, so the last registered subscription runs first. [3]
- If a subscription returns `true`, earlier-registered subscriptions are not called. [3]
- If no subscription returns `true`, or none are registered, the default back behavior is invoked and the app exits. [3]
- If a `Modal` is open, `BackHandler` publishes no events. See the `Modal` docs on `onRequestClose`. [3]

## With React Navigation

If you use React Navigation, the docs point to its guide on [Custom Android back button behaviour](https://reactnavigation.org/docs/custom-android-back-button-handling/). [2]

## In a native Android Fragment integration

If your host Activity is not a `ReactActivity`, it must implement `DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler` so React Native can handle back presses. [4]

Kotlin:

```kotlin
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
import com.facebook.react.modules.core.DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity(), DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler {

    override fun invokeDefaultOnBackPressed() {
        onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed()
    }
}
```
[4]

Java:

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import com.facebook.react.modules.core.DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler;

class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity implements DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler {

    @Override
    public void invokeDefaultOnBackPressed() {
        getOnBackPressedDispatcher().onBackPressed();
    }
}
```
[1]

`Activity.onBackPressed()` has been deprecated since API level 33. On Android 16 devices with apps targeting API level 36 it is no longer called, so use `OnBackPressedDispatcher` instead. [4]

## Not covered

The provided docs don't include a code example for registering a `BackHandler` listener (e.g. the specific method names and signatures).

React Native gives you two ways to separate code by platform: the `Platform` module and platform-specific file extensions [5].

## 1. `Platform` module

Use this when only small parts of a component differ [1].

`Platform.OS` is `ios` on iOS and `android` on Android [1]:

```tsx
import {Platform, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  height: Platform.OS === 'ios' ? 200 : 100,
});
```

[1]

`Platform.select` takes an object with keys `'ios' | 'android' | 'native' | 'default'` and returns the best match for the current platform. On a phone, `ios` and `android` take preference, then `native`, then `default` [1].

```tsx
const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    ...Platform.select({
      ios: {backgroundColor: 'red'},
      android: {backgroundColor: 'green'},
      default: {backgroundColor: 'blue'},
    }),
  },
});
```

[1]

Because it accepts any value, it can also return platform-specific components [1]:

```tsx
const Component = Platform.select({
  ios: () => require('ComponentIOS'),
  android: () => require('ComponentAndroid'),
})();

<Component />;
```

[1]

## 2. Platform-specific file extensions

Use this when the platform-specific code is more complex, and split it into separate files [2]. React Native detects `.ios.` and `.android.` extensions and loads the right file [2]:

```shell
BigButton.ios.js
BigButton.android.js
```

Then import without the platform extension:

```tsx
import BigButton from './BigButton';
```

React Native picks the correct file for the running platform [2].

Use `Alert.alert` with a title, a message, and an array of buttons. Each button's `onPress` fires when tapped, and tapping any button dismisses the alert [3].

```tsx
import {Button, Alert} from 'react-native';

const createTwoButtonAlert = () =>
  Alert.alert('Alert Title', 'My Alert Msg', [
    {
      text: 'Cancel',
      onPress: () => console.log('Cancel Pressed'),
      style: 'cancel',
    },
    {text: 'OK', onPress: () => console.log('OK Pressed')},
  ]);

// <Button title={'2-Button Alert'} onPress={createTwoButtonAlert} />
```
[1]

**Notes**

- If you don't pass any buttons, the alert shows a single "OK" button by default [3].
- On Android, a two-button alert is treated as "negative" and "positive" (such as Cancel, OK). Up to three buttons are allowed [2].
- Android alerts aren't dismissed by tapping outside the box by default. To enable that, pass `{cancelable: true}` as the fourth argument, and optionally handle it with `onDismiss` [2]:

```tsx
Alert.alert(
  'Alert Title',
  'My Alert Msg',
  [
    {
      text: 'Cancel',
      onPress: () => Alert.alert('Cancel Pressed'),
      style: 'cancel',
    },
  ],
  {
    cancelable: true,
    onDismiss: () =>
      Alert.alert('This alert was dismissed by tapping outside of the alert dialog.'),
  },
);
```
[2]

Use **`Pressable`** in React Native. Its `style` prop accepts a function that receives a `pressed` boolean and returns view styles, so you can change the appearance while the button is held down [1].

```tsx
<Pressable
  onPress={() => {
    setTimesPressed(current => current + 1);
  }}
  style={({pressed}) => [
    {
      backgroundColor: pressed ? 'rgb(210, 230, 255)' : 'white',
    },
    styles.wrapperCustom,
  ]}>
  {({pressed}) => (
    <Text style={styles.text}>{pressed ? 'Pressed!' : 'Press Me'}</Text>
  )}
</Pressable>
```
[3]

The children can also be a function that receives `pressed`, as in the example above, so you can change the content as well as the style [3].

The built-in `Button` component only supports a minimal level of customization, such as the `color` prop [2][4]. The docs suggest building your own button with `Pressable` if `Button` doesn't fit your app [4].

Generating a signed release build has two parts: configuring signing in Gradle, then building the AAB. The docs provided don't cover creating the upload key or setting the `MYAPP_UPLOAD_*` properties, so those steps are not covered here.

## 1. Add the signing config

Edit `android/app/build.gradle` so release builds are signed with your upload key [1]:

```groovy
...
android {
    ...
    defaultConfig { ... }
    signingConfigs {
        release {
            if (project.hasProperty('MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE')) {
                storeFile file(MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE)
                storePassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_PASSWORD
                keyAlias MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_ALIAS
                keyPassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_PASSWORD
            }
        }
    }
    buildTypes {
        release {
            ...
            signingConfig signingConfigs.release
        }
    }
}
...
```

## 2. Generate the release AAB

Run:

```shell
npx react-native build-android --mode=release
```

This uses Gradle's `bundleRelease` to bundle the JavaScript into the AAB [2]. The output is at `android/app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab`, ready to upload to Google Play [2].

Notes:
- Make sure `gradle.properties` doesn't contain `org.gradle.configureondemand=true`, or the release build will skip bundling JS and assets [2].
- App Signing by Google Play must be configured in the Google Play Console for it to accept the AAB format [2].
- Before uploading, you can test the release build with `npm run android -- --mode="release"` (or `yarn android --mode release`). This only works once signing is set up as above, and you should uninstall any previous version first [4].

