## React Native

Create an `Animated.Value` starting at 0, bind it to the `opacity` of an `Animated.View`, and run `Animated.timing` to 1 when the component mounts [1][3]:

```tsx
import {useEffect, useRef} from 'react';
import {Animated} from 'react-native';

const FadeInView = props => {
  const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current; // Initial value for opacity: 0

  useEffect(() => {
    Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
      toValue: 1,
      duration: 10000,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [fadeAnim]);

  return (
    <Animated.View
      style={{
        ...props.style,
        opacity: fadeAnim, // Bind opacity to animated value
      }}>
      {props.children}
    </Animated.View>
  );
};
```

The docs describe it this way: opacity starts at 0, then an easing animation updates the bound opacity on each frame until the value reaches 1 [1].

To trigger the fade from a button instead of on mount, call `Animated.timing(...).start()` inside a handler. The `Animated` example does this with `useAnimatedValue(0)` in place of `useRef` [2].

