}
```

`@Service` makes the service available throughout the app as a singleton [5]. It is shorthand for `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})` [1][5].

**Option 2: `@Injectable` with `providedIn: 'root'`**

Use this if you need constructor-based DI, advanced provider keys like `useClass`, or non-root scopes. Unlike `@Service`, `@Injectable` doesn't give you an implicit root singleton, so you must specify `{providedIn: 'root'}` [1].

**Option 3: Provide it at application bootstrap**

```ts
bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    LoggingService, // Used throughout the app
  ],
});
```

This gives you a true singleton [3]. Choose it when the provider has side-effects, requires configuration, or follows the `provideSomething` pattern [3]. The drawback is that it's always included in your bundle, even if never injected [3].

**Caution:** If you also list the service in a component's `providers`, that component gets its own instance instead of the shared one [2][4].

Add the `Validators.required` validator to the control.

1. Import `Validators` from `@angular/forms` [1]:

```ts
import {Validators} from '@angular/forms';
```

2. Add `Validators.required` as the second item in the control's array (here, the `firstName` control) [1]:

```ts
private formBuilder = inject(FormBuilder);

profileForm = this.formBuilder.group({
  firstName: ['', Validators.required],
  lastName: [''],
  address: this.formBuilder.group({
    street: [''],
    city: [''],
    state: [''],
    zip: [''],
  }),
});
```

3. Optionally, display the form status. A required field starts out invalid, and that status propagates to the parent form group, so you can read it from the group's `status` property [1]:

```html
<p>Form Status: {{ profileForm.status }}</p>
```

To show an error message, check `hasError('required')` on the control [5]:

```html
@if (name.invalid && (name.dirty || name.touched)) {
  @if (name.hasError('required')) {
    <div>Name is required.</div>
  }
}
```

Keep the `required` attribute in the template as well. It isn't necessary for validation, but it should be retained for accessibility [5].

Hydration works only for server-side rendered (SSR) applications, so enable SSR first by following the Angular SSR Guide. [1]

## With Angular CLI

If you enabled SSR through the CLI (during app creation or later via `ng add @angular/ssr`), the code that enables hydration should already be in your application. [1]

## Manual setup

For a custom setup, import `provideClientHydration` from `@angular/platform-browser` and add it to your bootstrapping providers. [1]

Standalone bootstrap:

```typescript
import {
  bootstrapApplication,
  provideClientHydration,
} from '@angular/platform-browser';
...

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration()]
});
```
[1]

With NgModules, add it to the root app module's providers:

```typescript
import {provideClientHydration} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [App],
  exports: [App],
  bootstrap: [App],
  providers: [provideClientHydration()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```
[1]

## Don't forget the server

The `provideClientHydration()` call must also be in the set of providers used to bootstrap the application on the **server**. [1][5] In projects with the default `ng new` structure, adding it to the root `AppModule` is enough, since the server module imports it. With a custom setup, add it to the providers list in the server bootstrap configuration. [1]
